  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "xo jacqui menopause support protein powder",
      "xo jacqui protein powder",
      "xo jacqui review",
      "xo jacqui cacao wild yam",
      "menopause protein powder",
      "wild yam protein powder",
      "chasteberry protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Menopause Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-protein-powder-for-menopause/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">XO Jacqui Menopause Support Protein Powder</Fragment>
      What is actually in XO Jacqui's menopause protein, what its wild yam and chasteberry can and cannot be expected to do, what it costs per gram, and who should talk to a doctor first.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#whats-in-it">What Is in It</a>
      <a href="#menopause-claims">Menopause Claims</a>
      <a href="#hrt-medications">HRT and Medications</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#is-15g-enough">Is 15 g Enough?</a>
      <a href="#taste-mixing">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs-alternatives">XO Jacqui vs Alternatives</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          XO Jacqui Menopause Support Protein Powder is an organic brown rice protein with six ingredients, including wild yam and chasteberry, that gives 15 g of protein per scoop and costs about $3.78 per 25 g at its $68 list price. Whether it suits you depends on how much protein you need and whether the botanicals are safe with your medications. Below: the label, the claims, testing, cost and alternatives.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. XO Jacqui Menopause Support Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            XO Jacqui Menopause Support Protein Powder is a clean, short-label organic rice protein, but at 15 g of protein a scoop and about $3.78 per 25 g it is a weak buy on protein alone, and its menopause claims rest on botanicals with thin evidence. The brand says the Cacao Wild Yam flavor contains brown rice protein, cacao, wild yam, beetroot, chasteberry and monk fruit, and nothing else.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want a dairy-free, soy-free, monk-fruit-sweetened powder with six ingredients, you like the flavor, and you plan to get most of your protein from meals anyway. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you want enough protein in one scoop to support muscle, if cost per gram matters, if you need independent testing, or if you take hormone therapy, birth control or dopamine-related medication and have not cleared chasteberry with your doctor.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">XO Jacqui by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">15 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop (brand)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~85</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories per 22 g serving (label data)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$68</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Brand price per tub</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$3.78</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cost per 25 g of protein</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            This review is built on the label, the brand's stated claims, published research on the ingredients and the prices we could see. We did not taste it. For the wider field, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-menopause/">best protein powder for menopause</a> guide ranks the options by protein dose, testing and cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. XO Jacqui and the Amazon Alternatives Worth Comparing
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first card is XO Jacqui's Cacao Wild Yam tub, the flavor with the most published label detail. The other four are Amazon alternatives that cover the jobs a menopause protein is usually bought for: more protein per scoop, organic plant protein for less money, independent testing, or soy. Prices are what we saw on Amazon on November 19, 2026, and they change.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="whats-in-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in XO Jacqui Menopause Support Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            XO Jacqui's Cacao Wild Yam powder contains six ingredients, per the brand and its Amazon listing: organic brown rice protein, cacao, wild yam, beetroot, chasteberry and monk fruit. There are no gums, oils, thickeners, added sugar or artificial sweeteners on the list, which is the product's strongest point. The brand also states that it is soy-free, gluten-free, GMO-free, estrogen-free and refined-sugar-free, and that it is made in Ojai, California.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brand puts protein at 15 g per serving. A food database entry built from the label shows a 22 g serving with about 85 calories, 4 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of fiber and 1 g of sugar. That makes the powder roughly 68 percent protein by weight, which is typical for a rice protein with flavorings and botanicals mixed in. The Amazon listing gives the tub as 1.45 lb, about 658 g, which lines up with the 30 servings a retailer listing gives. The brand's suggested serving is about a quarter cup; it says a larger scoop, about a third of a cup, brings the protein to roughly 21 g.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Flavor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ingredients (per the brand)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein source</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cacao Wild Yam</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brown rice protein, cacao, wild yam, beetroot, chasteberry, monk fruit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brown rice</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vanilla Greens</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brown rice protein, vanilla bean, sea moss, kale, wild yam, monk fruit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brown rice</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Berry Pomegranate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not confirmed; brand page did not load</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brown rice (brand line)</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The Vanilla Greens flavor swaps cacao, beetroot and chasteberry for vanilla bean, sea moss and kale, per the brand, and keeps the wild yam. That matters if chasteberry is the ingredient your doctor wants you to avoid: Vanilla Greens does not list it. XO Jacqui also sells a Berry Pomegranate menopause flavor, a cycle-support version for younger women, variety packs and bundles, but we could not load a full ingredient list for the berry flavors, so check the tub before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the label does not tell you is just as useful. We found no amino acid profile, so the leucine content is not published. We found no sodium or fat figure, and no full supplement-facts image we could read. The brand describes the formula as organic and the Amazon title says organic and vegan, but we did not find a USDA Organic certificate number for the product, so treat organic as the brand's claim. If you want a fully documented rice protein, our <a href="/best-brown-rice-protein-powder/">best brown rice protein powder</a> guide lists ones with complete panels.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="menopause-claims" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Does XO Jacqui Help With Menopause Symptoms?
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is no published trial of XO Jacqui itself, so any claim that it relieves hot flashes, mood swings, bloating or sleep problems is the brand's claim, not an established effect. XO Jacqui says its blend helps maintain bone density, muscle strength, balanced hormones and quality sleep during menopause, and that monk fruit supports stable blood sugar and digestion. Those are marketing statements. The fair way to judge the product is to look at each ingredient and what research says about it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The protein: useful, but the dose is small</h3>

          <p>
            Protein is the one ingredient with solid evidence behind it for women in midlife. Falling estrogen speeds up the loss of muscle and bone, and research reviews suggest older adults keep more muscle when they eat more protein than the basic adult allowance and spread it across meals, alongside strength training. Expert groups such as the PROT-AGE study group have suggested around 1.0 to 1.2 g per kg of body weight a day for healthy older adults. A 15 g scoop helps you get there, but it is a top-up rather than a full serving. Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> shows what your own target looks like.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wild yam: promoted for hormones, weak evidence</h3>

          <p>
            Wild yam contains diosgenin, a plant compound that labs can convert into hormones, which is why it is often marketed as natural progesterone. The human body cannot make that conversion on its own, and the small studies on wild yam for menopause symptoms have not shown a clear benefit. XO Jacqui also states the powder is estrogen-free, which is accurate as far as we can tell, but it means the wild yam should not be thought of as a hormone substitute.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Chasteberry: studied for PMS, less for menopause</h3>

          <p>
            Chasteberry, also called vitex, has been studied mostly for premenstrual symptoms. Evidence for menopause symptoms is limited and mixed, and the <a href="https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/chasteberry" rel="noopener">NIH's National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health</a> says it may affect hormone levels and should be avoided by some groups, which we cover in the next section. XO Jacqui does not state how much wild yam or chasteberry is in a serving, so even if you wanted to compare it with a study dose, you could not. Beetroot, cacao and monk fruit are flavor and color ingredients more than active ones. If symptom relief is your goal, the treatments with the strongest evidence, including hormone therapy and some non-hormonal prescriptions, are a conversation for your doctor, not a protein tub.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hrt-medications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is XO Jacqui Safe With HRT or Other Medications?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you take hormone therapy, birth control or any regular medication, ask your doctor or pharmacist before using the Cacao Wild Yam flavor, because it contains chasteberry, an herb that can affect hormones. The NIH's complementary health center says women who are pregnant, taking birth control pills or have a hormone-sensitive condition such as breast cancer should not use chasteberry, and that people taking dopamine-related drugs, including some antipsychotics and Parkinson's medicines, should avoid it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same caution reasonably extends to menopausal hormone therapy. HRT works by setting hormone levels on purpose, and adding an herb that may nudge them is something your prescriber should know about, even if the amount in a scoop is small. The trouble is that XO Jacqui does not publish the amount, so neither you nor your doctor can judge the dose. Bring the tub to your appointment or send a photo of the label.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Talk to a professional first</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">HRT, medications, kidney disease or a hormone-sensitive condition</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">See a doctor or registered dietitian before adding XO Jacqui if you take hormone therapy, birth control or dopamine-related medication, have had breast cancer or another hormone-sensitive condition, have kidney disease, or are pregnant. This review is not medical advice.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Kidney disease is the other group that needs advice first. People with reduced kidney function are often told to limit protein rather than add it, so any protein powder, including this one, should go through your kidney doctor or a renal dietitian. If you have a rice allergy, avoid it, and if you have diabetes, note that monk fruit does not raise blood sugar but the serving still carries a few grams of carbohydrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            For women who want a protein powder with nothing hormone-active in it at all, a plain protein is simpler. An unflavored whey isolate, an organic pea blend or XO Jacqui's own Vanilla Greens, which lists wild yam but not chasteberry, all remove the main interaction question. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-women/">best protein powder for women</a> guide lists straightforward options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is XO Jacqui Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            We found no evidence that XO Jacqui Menopause Support Protein Powder is third-party tested by a named program such as NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice, Informed Sport or the Clean Label Project. The brand describes the product as certified clean and 100 percent natural, but we could not find which organization certifies it, or a published certificate of analysis for heavy metals or contaminants.
          </p>

          <p>
            That gap matters more for rice protein than for most powders. Rice takes up arsenic, lead and cadmium from soil more readily than many crops, and independent tests of plant protein powders have repeatedly found higher heavy-metal levels in rice-based products than in whey. That does not mean XO Jacqui is contaminated; it means there is no published result either way. A brand charging $68 a tub could reasonably post its lab results.
          </p>

          <p>
            For comparison, Garden of Life Sport Plant-Based Protein is NSF Certified for Sport, per the brand, and Transparent Labs says its whey isolate is Informed Choice certified. Those programs test finished batches for banned substances, and NSF's program includes contaminant screening. If testing is a priority, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powders without heavy metals</a> guide focuses on brands that publish results.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own XO Jacqui and like it, the simplest step is to email the brand and ask for a certificate of analysis covering lead, arsenic, cadmium and mercury for your batch. Brands that test usually share it. If they will not, that is useful information too.
          </p>
        </div>

