        <div id="is-15g-enough" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is 15 g of Protein Enough for Women in Menopause?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fifteen grams is a useful snack-sized boost but usually less than one meal's worth for a woman over 50 trying to hold on to muscle. Research on older adults has generally found that muscle protein building responds best to about 25 to 30 g of high-quality protein at a meal, with roughly 2.5 to 3 g of leucine, the amino acid that acts as the trigger. A 15 g scoop of rice protein gets about halfway there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leucine is the weak spot. XO Jacqui does not publish an amino acid profile, but rice protein typically contains around 8 percent leucine, so a 15 g serving would provide roughly 1.2 g. That is an estimate from typical rice protein values, not a label figure. Whey isolate is closer to 10 to 11 percent leucine, which is why a 28 g scoop of whey reaches the threshold easily and a small scoop of rice protein does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can close the gap. Blending a scoop into a smoothie with a cup of soy milk or Greek yogurt adds 7 to 15 g more protein. Using the brand's larger scoop gives about 21 g, though the tub empties faster. Or treat XO Jacqui as the protein in an afternoon snack and rely on meals for the rest of the day. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> walks through daily targets by weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            The low dose has an upside for some buyers. At about 85 calories a serving, it fits into a weight-management plan without much effort, and some women find a small shake easier to finish than a 30 g one. If appetite is low, as it can be later in life, a smaller drink taken twice a day can be more realistic than one big shake. That is a fair reason to choose it, as long as you know you are buying a top-up.
          </p>
        </div>

